Sure, here's an example implementation of an observer pattern in Rust based on the RxJS library:

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::collections::HashMap;

type EventHandler<T> = Arc<Mutex<Box<dyn FnMut(T)>>>;

struct Observable<T> {
    observers: HashMap<String, EventHandler<T>>,
}

impl<T> Observable<T> {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Observable {
            observers: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    fn subscribe(&mut self, name: &str, handler: EventHandler<T>) {
        self.observers.insert(String::from(name), handler);
    }

    fn unsubscribe(&mut self, name: &str) {
        self.observers.remove(name);
    }

    fn notify(&mut self, data: T) {
        for (_, handler) in self.observers.iter_mut() {
            handler.lock().unwrap()(data.clone());
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a generic Observable struct that stores a list of observers as EventHandler closures. The subscribe method adds a new observer to the list with a given name, and the unsubscribe method removes an observer by name. The notify method sends data to all the registered observers, triggering their respective handlers.

To use this observer pattern in your Rust code, you can create a new instance of Observable, subscribe to it using subscribe, and then call notify with data whenever you want to notify the observers. For example:

```rust
let mut observable = Observable::<i32>::new();

let handler1 = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Box::new(|data| {
    println!("Observer 1 received data: {}", data);
})));
observable.subscribe("observer1", handler1);

let handler2 = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Box::new(|data| {
    println!("Observer 2 received data: {}", data);
})));
observable.subscribe("observer2", handler2);

observable.notify(42);

observable.unsubscribe("observer1");
observable.notify(13);
```

This code creates a new Observable that emits i32 data, subscribes two observers with different handlers, notifies the observers with data, unsubscribes one observer, and then notifies the remaining observer with new data.

In the Quasar framework, you can pass data from one component to another by using props.

Here's how you can pass data from a parent component to a child component using props:

1) In the parent component, define a prop with a name and a type:

```php
<template>
  <child-component :propName="parentData"></child-component>
</template>

<script>
import ChildComponent from 'path/to/ChildComponent.vue'

export default {
  components: {
    ChildComponent
  },
  data() {
    return {
      parentData: 'data to pass'
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

2) In the child component, declare the prop with the same name:

```php
<template>
  <div>{{propName}}</div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  props: {
    propName: String
  }
}
</script>
```

In this example, we passed a string as the prop type, but you can also use other data types like numbers or objects.

You can also pass data from a child component to a parent component using events. Here's how you can emit an event from a child component and listen to it in a parent component:

1) In the child component, define a method that emits an event:

```php
<template>
  <button @click="emitEvent">Emit Event</button>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  methods: {
    emitEvent() {
      this.$emit('eventName', 'data to pass')
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

2) In the parent component, listen to the event and define a method to handle it:

```php
<template>
  <child-component @eventName="handleEvent"></child-component>
</template>

<script>
import ChildComponent from 'path/to/ChildComponent.vue'

export default {
  components: {
    ChildComponent
  },
  methods: {
    handleEvent(data) {
      console.log(data) // 'data to pass'
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

In this example, we emitted an event with a name "eventName" and passed a string as data. The parent component listened to the "eventName" event and defined a method "handleEvent" to handle the data.

For your specific task, a Transformer-based language model such as BERT, GPT-2 or RoBERTa would be a good choice. When picking a model, you should consider the following factors:
The size of the model, as larger models tend to have better performance but are also more computationally expensive to fine-tune;
The domain specificity of the pre-training data, as models trained on academic papers and code may not perform well on other types of text;
The pre-training objective, as models trained for language generation tasks (e.g. GPT-2) may not perform well on tasks that require a more structured understanding of text (e.g. question answering);
The availability of pre-trained weights and fine-tuning scripts, as these will make it easier to get started with your fine-tuning process.
You can find pre-trained language models from a variety of sources, including academic papers, research institutions, and commercial organizations. A good starting point for finding pre-trained models is the Hugging Face Model Hub (https://huggingface.co/models), which has a comprehensive list of pre-trained models and code for fine-tuning. You can also use a search engine (e.g. Google) to find pre-trained models by searching for specific models (e.g. "BERT pre-trained model") or by searching for general lists of pre-trained models (e.g. "list of pre-trained language models").

A few options for pre-trained models that you could consider include:

BERT: BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) is a widely-used pre-trained model that has achieved state-of-the-art performance on a variety of NLP tasks. Advantages of BERT include its ability to handle context-dependent word representations, as well as its pre-training on a large corpus of text. However, BERT may not be the best choice for your task, as it was pre-trained on a diverse set of text rather than specifically on academic papers and code.
RoBERTa: RoBERTa (Robustly Optimized BERT Approach) is an optimized version of BERT that has achieved even better performance on a variety of NLP tasks. Advantages of RoBERTa include its larger size and more optimized pre-training process. However, like BERT, RoBERTa was not specifically pre-trained on academic papers and code, so its performance on your task may not be optimal.
ALBERT: ALBERT (A Lite BERT) is a lighter version of BERT that has been optimized for computationally-efficient fine-tuning. Advantages of ALBERT include its small size and efficient fine-tuning process, making it a good choice if you have limited computational resources. However, its smaller size may also result in lower performance compared to BERT or RoBERTa.

